{"graph_chars": "Bring it home: organic food. Sales went from one billion dollars in nineteen ninety to nearly sixty-two billion in twenty twenty. Tastes shifted toward safer food. Incomes rose. Population grew. Demand shifted right, and we moved up the supply curve. More farmers also converted to organic, so supply shifted right too. Quantity is definitely higher. Price falls only if supply shifts more than demand. Organic methods still cost more, so the gap may never fully close. Strawberries on the Dirty Dozen list in two thousand thirteen shifted demand for organic strawberries right: higher price and higher quantity. Recitation. Demand is the curve. Quantity demanded is a point. Law of demand: price up, quantity demanded down. Supply is the curve. Quantity supplied is a point. Law of supply: price up, quantity supplied up. Equilibrium is where they cross. Above it, surplus. Below it, shortage. Ceteris paribus. Shifters are not own-price. Four steps. A ceiling below equilibrium makes a shortage. A floor above it makes a surplus. Consumer plus producer surplus is social surplus, largest at equilibrium. Deadweight loss is the inefficiency of blocking trades. That is Chapter Three.", "graph_times": [[0.0, 0.0684], [0.0684, 0.1368], [0.1368, 0.2052], [0.2052, 0.2736], [0.2736, 0.3421], [0.3421, 0.4105], [0.4105, 0.4789], [0.4789, 0.5473], [0.5473, 0.6157], [0.6157, 0.6841], [0.6841, 0.7525], [0.7525, 0.8209], [0.8209, 0.8894], [0.8894, 0.9578], [0.9578, 1.0262], [1.0262, 1.0946], [1.0946, 1.163], [1.163, 1.2314], [1.2314, 1.2998], [1.2998, 1.3682], [1.3682, 1.4367], [1.4367, 1.5051], [1.5051, 1.5735], [1.5735, 1.6419], [1.6419, 1.7103], [1.7103, 1.7787], [1.7787, 1.8471], [1.8471, 1.9155], [1.9155, 1.984], [1.984, 2.0524], [2.0524, 2.1208], [2.1208, 2.1892], [2.1892, 2.2576], [2.2576, 2.326], [2.326, 2.3944], [2.3944, 2.4628], [2.4628, 2.5312], [2.5312, 2.5997], [2.5997, 2.6681], [2.6681, 2.7365], [2.7365, 2.8049], [2.8049, 2.8733], [2.8733, 2.9417], [2.9417, 3.0101